<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The architecture-test suite ADR-0024 names by this exact project name, and
    the row design 20 section 3.1 gives it in the taxonomy. It is the first
    project under `tests/`; that folder held nothing but a placeholder until
    2026-08-02.

    TargetFramework reads $(NamiApplicationTargetFramework), the SINGLE-target
    knob, and this is a choice with no source rather than a rule being followed.
    ADR-0030 parameter B splits the two knobs by what a thing is: a published
    library multi-targets, "the host is single-target on the current LTS". A
    test project is neither, and no document in this repository says which knob
    it takes. It reads the application one because under the testing platform
    this project genuinely is an executable, and because multi-targeting it
    would run the same assertions once per framework for no added signal. Revisit
    when .NET 12 ships and the two knobs stop being the same string, which is the
    first moment the choice can be wrong in a visible way.

    OutputType is Exe because the test project is its own runner: xunit.v3 ships
    Microsoft Testing Platform support, and with TestingPlatformDotnetTestSupport
    set, `dotnet test` drives it directly. Measured on 2026-08-02, SDK 10.0.301:
    with only the two package references below, `dotnet test` reported
    "Passed! - Failed: 0, Passed: 2" and a deliberately broken rule exited 1.
    So Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk and xunit.runner.visualstudio are BOTH absent on
    purpose. Adding them is not neutral: the same probe measured 28 restore-graph
    nodes with the pair against 23 without, and every added node is a licence
    read owed under ADR-0026.

    IsPackable is false. Nothing under `tests/` is published (ADR-0027 ships the
    packages design 01 section 3.1 lists, and this is not one of them).

    There are no PublicAPI.*.txt files here and there must not be. ADR-0044
    parameter A locks the surface of "every public package"; this project is not
    a package, and the analyzer is referenced per-project in `src/` rather than
    inherited, so nothing asks for them.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>$(NamiApplicationTargetFramework)</TargetFramework>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <TestingPlatformDotnetTestSupport>true</TestingPlatformDotnetTestSupport>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3" />
    <PackageReference Include="TngTech.ArchUnitNET.xUnitV3" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The ProjectReference is what gives the test the assembly to inspect, and it
    does NOT weaken the rule being tested: the arrow points from the test into
    Abstractions, never the other way, and design 01 section 3.1 constrains what
    Abstractions depends on rather than what may depend on it.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/Nami.Identity.Abstractions/Nami.Identity.Abstractions.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
